{"content":"Converting GIF to video yields dramatic savings: a 3.7MB GIF can shrink to 551KB as MP4 or 341KB as WebM (roughly 85-90% reduction) with equal or better visual quality since video codecs use inter-frame compression GIF lacks. Recommended pattern: use a <video> element with autoplay muted loop playsinline, providing WebM source first (best compression, wide modern browser support) and MP4 fallback (universal support). FFmpeg commands: 'ffmpeg -i in.gif -c vp9 -b:v 0 -crf 41 out.webm' and 'ffmpeg -i in.gif -b:v 0 -crf 25 -vcodec libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p out.mp4'. Native lazy-loading (loading=lazy) works on <img>/<iframe> but video needs manual intersection-observer-based lazy loading since the attribute doesn't apply to <video>.","source":"web","provider":"websearch","confidence":"MEDIUM","fetched_at":"2026-07-21T14:19:25.059Z","ttl":86400000,"kind":"web"}
